tes and filter. For example, if a sprite needs a 2-pixel contact edge, grow it from those 2 pixels — don't generate random sprites and hope one matches. The only acceptable rejection loop is the outer `_gen_attempt` retry + final `main(inp) == out` oracle check. Any inner loop with > 50% rejection rate means the approach is wrong — restructure to construct the desired output directly.\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:69:3. **Output-first means output-first in code structure**: When the generation_strategy in spec says \\\"output-first\\\" approach, your code must literally follow the generation_strategy steps to build the full output first (typically the more constrained structure), then derive the input (the easier thing) from it by undoing the solver's transformation described in `main()`. Finally, validate with `np.array_equal(main(input), output)`. You are doing it wrong if your code places input objects first and hopes the solver produces a good output by random trial, which often has near-zero acceptance rate.\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:72:6. **Code structure**: split into `_gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None` (single attempt, returns grid or None on failure) and `generate_input()` (retry loop, max 300 attempts). This keeps generation logic flat and readable.\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/generator-writer.md:119:Append `generate_input()` (and any helper functions it needs) to `/home/xd/projects/BARC/solvers/arc2/<task_id>.py` (or to the temp file if `attempt_i` was provided).\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:10:1. Guide a separate LLM agent to write a `generate_input()` function\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/skill/BARC/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md:23:- A generator author can implement `generate_input()` purely from this spec\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/.gitignore:15:!/ceshi/code_combo/\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/.gitignore:16:!/ceshi/c